In the rest of this section, I conduct simulations to show the change in the 
dependent variable holding all else constant and manipulating an independent 
variable of interest. I then plot the result to give a visual representation of 
the findings of the models above. While this type of simulation does not represent 
the interrelated nature of the independent variables in the models above, it does 
allow me to demonstrate the maximum possible effect predicted by the models 
described above -- assuming the independent variable is changing while all other
variables remain constant.\footnote{Simulations are conducted using a modification 
of the \texttt{sim} function in the \texttt{arm} package for R.}

In addition to demonstrating the effect of the key variables of interest, I use 
simulations to illustrate the election and school district specific effects to 
provide a sense of the scale of year to year and district to district variation 
captured in the models. This simulation technique presents caterpillar plots of 
the estimated values of the impact the unobservable characteristics of these 
grouping variables have on turnout and rolloff. 

\clearpage

\subsubsection{Turnout Models}


<<fixefplot1, eval=FALSE, fig.cap="Coefficient Plot for Full Model Fixed Effects",fig.height=8.5, fig.width=8, fig.show='hide'>>=
set.seed(51315)
sim1 <- FEsim(mixedMod1lagsb, nsims = 1000); sim1$dv <- "Lags"
sim2 <- FEsim(mixedModcloseC, nsims = 1000); sim2$dv <- "Close"
sim3 <- FEsim(fullmodMMd, nsims = 1000); sim3$dv <- "Defeat"
sim4 <- FEsim(fullmodMMblq, nsims = 1000); sim4$dv <- "Competitiveness"

simPlot <- do.call(rbind, list(sim1, sim2, sim3, sim4))
simPlot$dv <- factor(simPlot$dv, levels = c("No Incumb.", "Contest", 
                                            "Defeat", "Competitiveness"))
alpha <- 1.96
simPlot$upr <- simPlot$meanEff + (alpha*simPlot$sdEff)
simPlot$lwr <- simPlot$meanEff - (alpha*simPlot$sdEff)

simPlot$color <- "Not Sig."
simPlot$color <- ifelse(simPlot$lwr >0 | simPlot$upr < 0, "Sig", "Not Sig.")

ggplot(simPlot, aes(x = variable, y = meanEff, ymax = upr, 
                    ymin = lwr, color = color)) + 
  scale_color_brewer(palette = "Dark2") + 
  geom_point() + geom_errorbar(h=1.5) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, size = I(1.1), color = I("black"), linetype = 2, 
             alpha = I(0.6)) +
  coord_flip() + 
  facet_wrap(~dv) + 
  theme_dpi(base_size=12) +theme(legend.position = "bottom") + 
  labs(x = "Variable", y = "Effect", color = "Stat. Sig.")

# confint(fullmodMMni, method = "Wald", level = 0.9)
@

Figure \ref{fig:fixefSub1} shows the simulated effect of four variables on turnout. 
These plots show four randomly sampled 
observations (represented by a different colored line) with the independent 
variable on the x-axis and the predicted school board turnout on the y-axis. Along 
the x-axis is a rug-plot showing the relative density of observations at each 
range of the x-variable to demonstrate where the model is extrapolating 
with less data. The two dashed horizontal lines shown represent the inter-quartile range 
of the predicted values. This is presented to provide 
a reference for how powerful the effect of the independent variable -- as shown 
by the slope of the line -- is relative to the overall variability in turnout. 

In Figure \ref{fig:fixefSub1} the modest effects in Table \ref{tab:turnoutRESULTS} 
are illustrated. The negative effect for the teacher share of voters 
on turnout in the top right panel show the modest impact interest groups may play 
in school board votes. The positive effect of competitiveness on turnout is 
depicted in the bottom left. On the other 
diagonal there is an expected relationship between prior turnout and current 
turnout as positive and fairly strong. Partisan division has no measurable effect 
on turnout. 

Taken together with the results in Table \ref{tab:turnoutRESULTS} it seems clear 
that net of election cycle and district long-term trends, as well as demographic 
characteristics of the communities, school board election turnout is relatively 
unmoved by these factors. 


